Many small businesses keep customer records in Salesforce and spend their day in Microsoft 365, so an announcement that joins the two deserves a careful read. On 17 November 2026, at Microsoft Ignite, Microsoft and Salesforce announced a partnership called Copilotforce. This post reads the joint press release and Microsoft's Copilot blog post from the same event and sorts out what exists today, what is a preview, and what neither company has said.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Microsoft's Copilot offers Anthropic and OpenAI models and Microsoft's own page compares Copilot with Claude and ChatGPT on cost, so read that claim as Microsoft's. Stackbrook did not test Copilotforce or any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated.*

## What Copilotforce is

According to the press release, Copilotforce brings Salesforce customer context, business processes and actions into Microsoft Copilot, Teams and Fabric. The idea is that a salesperson can ask Copilot about a deal and get an answer that draws on both Salesforce records and the work context Microsoft holds, without switching apps. Microsoft calls that context layer Work IQ, which pulls from Teams meetings, Outlook email and SharePoint or OneDrive documents.

The release describes several separate pieces, and they are at very different stages. That is the key point for anyone deciding whether to plan around it.

## What is available and what is not

The announcement says the Salesforce in Copilot plugin entered public preview (beta) on the day of the announcement. It ships with more than 38 prebuilt sales skills, and the named examples include Pipeline Review, Close Planning, Deal Review, Customer Health, Renewal Radar and Call Follow-Up. Sellers can reach customer data and take supported actions through authenticated Salesforce MCP access, within their existing Salesforce permissions. The release says supported writeback actions to the CRM need explicit user confirmation and respect existing validation rules.

Sales Cloud in Teams is further back. It is in pilot with enterprise customers, with an open beta planned for "later this year" according to the release. It is described as showing pipeline and quota progress, deal notifications and briefing materials inside Teams.

On the data side, Data Federation from Microsoft Fabric OneLake into Salesforce Data 360 is described as generally available. It lets joint customers query supported OneLake data inside Data 360 without moving it. A shortcut running the other direction, from Data 360 into OneLake, is in public preview. A two-way Zero Copy integration between Data 360 and Fabric is described only as something the companies are bringing, with no stage given.

![Stage board of the five Copilotforce components, from generally available to direction only](/images/site1/copilotforce-salesforce-microsoft-explained-chart.svg)
