## Who is involved

Accenture is named as design and implementation partner and as a pilot customer, and the release quotes its chief executive describing Accenture as "customer zero." The release cites Accenture figures of roughly 814,000 people, 13 industries, 55,000 Salesforce users and Copilot scaled to more than 740,000 people. Those are Accenture and vendor figures from the release, not independent measurements.

## The wider Copilot announcements

Microsoft's Copilot blog post from the same day covers more than Salesforce. Per the post, Home (Chat and Cowork combined), Office in Copilot and Copilot Managed Runtime are generally available now, while Copilot Code is due to reach general availability "in the coming weeks." Autopilot, an always-on agent with its own Microsoft Entra agent identity and a named manager, is rolling out to Frontier customers. Workspaces is available in Frontier, and a command-center experience called Today is in private preview.

For people who use Dynamics 365 rather than Salesforce, the post says updated Sales, Customer Service and Finance and Operations plugins bring more than 100 prebuilt skills into Copilot. It lists 44 sales skills, 13 customer service skills and 30 finance skills, and the page does not break the 100-plus total down further. It separately mentions more than 20 legal skills. It also says Agent 365 now gives visibility across platforms including Anthropic, Databricks, OpenAI and Salesforce, with new controls over MCP servers, plugins and skills.

Microsoft says Office in Copilot, which creates and co-authors Word, Excel and PowerPoint files inside the Copilot app, is "already included in your Copilot subscription license." The post describes the commercial model for Copilot as a per-user subscription plus usage-based consumption for the most demanding tasks.

## The cost claim to treat with care

The Copilot post cites a GigaOm report and says that, for comparable quality, Copilot is 84 percent cheaper than Claude and 63 percent cheaper than ChatGPT for an everyday user, and 68 percent and 35 percent cheaper for an advanced user. This is Microsoft's own summary of a report it cites. We did not read the underlying report, so these figures are vendor-cited and unverified, and we cannot say how it defined everyday use, which plans it compared or how it measured quality. Until you can read that method yourself, treat the numbers as a vendor claim rather than a finding.

## What neither page says

Neither page gives prices or licence terms for the Salesforce in Copilot plugin, including whether it needs a separate licence. Microsoft does say Office in Copilot is already included in a Copilot subscription licence, but neither page names the specific subscription edition needed for the rest. They do not say which Microsoft 365 Copilot plans or Salesforce editions are required, or what usage limits apply. There is no general availability date for the Salesforce plugin or Sales Cloud in Teams. The press release says regional availability and customer access are governed by applicable customer agreements, which means you would need to check your own contracts. Microsoft's Autopilot and Copilot Code pricing are also unstated.

## A cautious way to approach it

This is general information, not legal or financial advice. For a small team already on both products, a reasonable first step is to read your current Salesforce and Microsoft agreements and ask your account contacts about licensing for the preview items. Because the plugin is a beta, try it first on non-critical records, and keep the confirmation step on for any writeback to your CRM. Stackbrook has not tested any of this, so each capability above is the vendors' description, not a verified result.
